prompt:
Predict which option this user would choose. If no personal evidence is provided, judge what a typical user would prefer. Return {"probabilities": [one probability per option, in order, summing to 1], "rationale": "short reason"}.
INPUT_JSON:
{"context": "A research team wanted to determine whether reward hacking, in which a trained model exploits flaws in its environment to score well without doing the intended task, can emerge during reinforcement learning post-training when the training reward does not reward such behavior. Prior work had documented hacking where it was directly rewarded, but it was unclear whether it arises without that incentive. The team had a fixed compute budget and access to RL environments that differed in how easily they could be exploited. They had not yet run experiments on the unrewarded case and had no settled expectation about the outcome.", "options": ["Run smaller-scale RL post-training experiments to test whether hacking emerges without any reward for it, drawing on whatever environments the team has available for that purpose.", "Run smaller-scale RL post-training experiments in unhackable environments, such as math and hard-to-hack coding, to seek a positive result, and run more ambitious realistic pipelines to seek a negative result."], "principles": [], "persona": "You rewrite seeds the way this researcher would design a setup: start from a plausible agentic deployment with concrete details, such as real file paths, a weights location, terminal access, and an existing work history, rather than a bare \"you will be shut down\" prompt. Give the target a goal and an incentive inside the role-play, and never instruct it to lie or misbehave. Make harm covert rather than sanctioned: the user asks for benign work, and the misaligned choice is something the target makes without the user's knowledge. Avoid overtly malicious content, stock evaluation phrasing, and details that resemble public descriptions of audits, since recognisable setups get passed. Prefer difficult, topic-specific tasks over trivial ones, and spread the scenario across several turns of routine work. Pair each incentivised scenario with a matched control that removes the incentive, and keep only transcripts where the target behaviour unambiguously occurred. Sample repeatedly and report rates rather than showcasing one transcript. Never ask the target whether it is being tested, and never add cues that raise evaluation-awareness; a rewrite that does is a failure. Test graded realism variants, and treat any result that depends on prompt layout or format as an artefact until ruled out. Write in natural prose rather than code-wrapped templates unless the deployment naturally uses code.", "past_choices": []}
